# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: a check of the settings files, a search for switched-off
checks, rustfmt, Clippy, the dependency rules, a build with warnings as errors, and the tests,
which must all run. The git hook and CI run it too. It runs under `mise exec` on its own, so it
needs no prefix.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/check`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.claude/`, `rust-toolchain.toml`, `mise.toml`,
  `mise.lock`, `rustfmt.toml`, `clippy.toml`, `deny.toml`, `.config/nextest.toml`, the `[lints]`
  table and `rust-version` in `Cargo.toml`, and existing tests.
- `scripts/check` rejects other settings: a table in `Cargo.toml` other than `[package]`, the
  dependency tables, `[features]` and `[lints]` (so no `[profile]`, `[workspace]`, `[[test]]`
  or path dependency), a `build.rs`, another mise or rustfmt config file, and `.cargo/config.toml`.

```sh
mise exec -- cargo nextest run banner                         # tests whose name contains "banner"
mise exec -- cargo test --doc word_count                      # one doc example
mise exec -- cargo fmt                                        # format
mise exec -- cargo clippy --fix --allow-dirty --all-targets   # apply Clippy's safe fixes
```

Sandboxed agents: cargo writes to `~/.cargo`, rustup to `~/.rustup`, and mise to
`~/.local/share/mise`, `~/.local/state/mise` and its cache (`~/Library/Caches/mise` on macOS,
`~/.cache/mise` on Linux). The first run needs the network (crates.io, static.rust-lang.org,
GitHub release downloads), and `gh` always does. Without these, `scripts/check` fails for reasons
outside the code.

## Rust

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing Rust.

The checks enforce the `[lints]` list in `Cargo.toml`: no `unsafe`; no `unwrap`, `expect`,
`panic!`, `unreachable!`, `todo!`, `dbg!` or `process::exit` outside tests; a doc comment on every
public item and on each crate. `#[allow]` and `#[expect]` are rejected, even with a reason: fix the
cause, or ask the owner for an exception in `Cargo.toml`. Also rejected: `#[doc(hidden)]`,
`_ = f();`, proptest settings in the code, and doc examples marked `ignore`, `no_run` or
`should_panic`. Every `#[test]` function must run: a cfg that hides one turns the check red.
Prefix commands with `mise exec --` when `cargo nextest` is not found.

## Layout

- Logic goes in the library: `src/lib.rs` and modules `src/<name>.rs`. `src/main.rs` only calls
  it.
- Tests of private functions go in a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` at the end of their module. Tests of
  the public API go in `tests/`, one file per area.
- Commit `Cargo.lock`, and any `proptest-regressions/` file a failing property writes.
- `rust-toolchain.toml` names the Rust version. Bump it together with `rust-version` in
  `Cargo.toml`.
- After changing `mise.toml`, run `mise install` and commit `mise.lock`.
- One crate until the owner approves a workspace.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
